From 876a21abaf14d1692cdf4e10e8eb550db0d2462c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Alessandro Fontana <alessandro.fontana@pm.me>
Date: Fri, 14 Aug 2026 20:55:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] extcon: sm5502: only re-read the state for coalesced
 interrupts

Resolving the cable state by reading the hardware fixes a coalesced
attach/detach pair, but it also throws away the information carried by a
lone detach.  The work item runs some milliseconds after the interrupt,
and by then the chip may still be reporting the cable it has just lost:
sm5502_muic_get_cable_type() succeeds, the handler is called with
attached = true, and the detach never reaches the extcon device.

On a Galaxy Tab A (SM-T580) unplugging a CDP port leaves the extcon
device reporting CDP=1 indefinitely while power_supply online has already
gone to 0.  The muic-detach interrupt is counted, so the event does
arrive and is serviced - it is simply overridden.

Ask the hardware only when the two flags disagree, which is the case the
re-read exists for, and take a lone flag at face value.
---
 drivers/extcon/extcon-sm5502.c | 22 ++++++++++++++--------
 1 file changed, 14 insertions(+), 8 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/extcon/extcon-sm5502.c b/drivers/extcon/extcon-sm5502.c
index 4d422e1f13..7933db77c1 100644
--- a/drivers/extcon/extcon-sm5502.c
+++ b/drivers/extcon/extcon-sm5502.c
@@ -584,22 +584,28 @@ static void sm5502_muic_irq_work(struct work_struct *work)
 	/*
 	 * Detect attached or detached cables.
 	 *
-	 * Both flags can be set in the same run, in which case replaying
-	 * them in order would leave the state of whichever came last.
-	 * Read the current cable type instead: a negative return means
-	 * nothing identifiable is connected.
+	 * Both flags can be set in the same run, in which case replaying them
+	 * in order would leave the state of whichever came last.  Ask the
+	 * hardware instead: a negative return means nothing identifiable is
+	 * connected.  A single flag is taken as it is, because by the time
+	 * this runs the chip may still be reporting a cable that has just
+	 * been unplugged.
 	 */
-	if (info->irq_attach || info->irq_detach) {
+	if (info->irq_attach && info->irq_detach) {
 		bool attached;
 
 		attached = sm5502_muic_get_cable_type(info) >= 0;
 
 		ret = sm5502_muic_cable_handler(info, attached);
-
-		info->irq_attach = false;
-		info->irq_detach = false;
+	} else if (info->irq_attach) {
+		ret = sm5502_muic_cable_handler(info, true);
+	} else if (info->irq_detach) {
+		ret = sm5502_muic_cable_handler(info, false);
 	}
 
+	info->irq_attach = false;
+	info->irq_detach = false;
+
 	if (ret < 0)
 		dev_err(info->dev, "failed to handle MUIC interrupt\n");
 
-- 
